A local round, and one page anyone can find

Two things the framework described but did not ship: a runnable loop, and a place to read
the result. Everything here is additive — no existing behaviour changes, `run.sh` and the
workflow are untouched, and a repo that adopts none of it keeps working exactly as now.

## What this is for

I want to run the council **on my own machine, on a clock**, rather than on the pipeline —
not because the pipeline is wrong, but because `hosted` is the metered API and I would
rather spend the subscription I already have. That is the third path `LOCAL.md` has always
described in prose ("a desktop routine, an agent on your laptop, or you") and never handed
anybody as a command.

## `bin/round.sh` — the routine prompt, made executable

Fetch, run every seat through **the same `run.sh` that CI calls** (so the two paths cannot
drift), hand each open work order to a local agent, commit, push, publish.

```sh
node .advocate-engine/bin/round.sh              # a whole round
node .advocate-engine/bin/round.sh --dry-run    # what is owed; summon nothing
node .advocate-engine/bin/round.sh --seat upkeep
```

It refuses the way everything else here does: **if the agent does not delete the work order
and leave a session note, the workspace is reverted and the seat still reads as owed.** A
round that fired is not a round that spoke. The agent is swappable via
`ADVOCATE_LOCAL_AGENT` — this must not become a Claude Code framework — and the bundled
default is the `claude` CLI at `ADVOCATE_MODEL` (default `sonnet`, because a caretaking
session is reading, and the cheap one is the one you can afford weekly).

## The workspace had to move out of `.git`, and that is the interesting finding

The first real local session **died on its first write**, which is the best argument for
having built this at all.

`session.mjs` parks each seat's worktree at `.git/advocate-work/<seat>`, and the comment
explains why: a workspace under the repo root gets swept into a stray `git add -A` and
committed to `main`. That reason is sound. But Claude Code treats the entire `.git` directory
as a sensitive path and refuses to write there — unattended, with nobody to approve it — so
**the framework's headline story, "a local agent picks up the work order", could not be walked
by the most likely local agent.** It had never been run, so nobody had found out.

Outside the repo is the *stronger* version of the original guarantee: it cannot be committed
to the subject by accident, because it is not in the subject. `session.mjs` honours
`ADVOCATE_WORK_DIR` and is otherwise byte-identical in behaviour, so **CI is untouched** and
keeps its `.git` default; `round.sh` sets it to `~/.local/state/advocate`.

## `bin/digest.mjs` + `bin/publish.sh` — the reporting gap

A branch per seat is right for the work and **wrong for the reader**. Nobody checks out five
branches to find out whether anything happened, which means the framework's actual output
was, in practice, unread.

So a round ends by rewriting one page: every seat, when it last spoke, drafts still forming,
what is asking to graduate, what is owed.

```yaml
report:
  branch: council      # default. `report: false` publishes nothing.
  wiki: false
```

Three choices worth arguing with:

- **Whole, never appended, no archive.** Reports get edited far more than they get written
  and the value of the current one is that it is current. The history is in the branch,
  which is where history goes. I did not want a tower of dated reports nobody reads.
- **An orphan branch, not `main`.** A generated page in `main` churns the default branch
  every round, and then every submodule pin pointing at that repo looks stale when nothing
  changed. `/tree/council` renders `README.md`, so the branch *is* the page.
- **Every destination is a push.** No issue, no discussion, no REST call. Not squeamishness:
  a second surface is a second thing that can be stale while the repository is fine, and an
  agent that needed a token and an HTTP client to say what it thinks is harder to replace
  than one that only knows how to commit.

`report.wiki: true` is opt-in for a **mechanical** reason I checked rather than assumed:
GitHub does not create `<repo>.wiki.git` until a first page exists, so on a repo whose wiki
was never opened there is no remote to push to at all (`git ls-remote` → *Repository not
found*, even though `has_wiki` is true in the API). One click in a browser, once, and then it
is only ever a push. `publish.sh` says exactly that if you turn it on too early.

## `METHOD.md` — two things it did not say

**How much to do in one session.** There was no answer, which invites the two bad ones:
fixed quota, or as much as fits. The rule now is that the range sets the floor, the seat sets
the ceiling and spends it on depth, and it tries to finish **one thing more** than last time —
ripen a draft, close a complaint, measure a goal it reported as unmeasured. A session whose
only product is one draft moving is a good session. Length is not effort.

**A seat never proposes a seat.** Seating is an external gesture by the repository's owners.
An advocate that could seat advocates would be a thing nobody appointed appointing things —
and the softer version, quietly adopting a concern nobody gave it, is the same failure with
better manners. Noticing a gap is still free and useful: one line in the session note, flagged
as outside every seat. A raised hand, not a claim staked. Same line on remedies — say what the
condition is and what it would take; do not strategise the migration. If the owners want the
alternative, **that decision becomes a new advocacy, and it is theirs to make.**

## A correction in `LOCAL.md`

It said **"Nothing about the repository is sent to an inference provider"** — flatly, in bold —
three paragraphs above a routine prompt that hands the work to an agent. Both cannot be true.

Rewritten to the claim that *is* true and is worth more anyway: **the pipeline never calls
out.** What drains the queue is the operator's choice, on their terms, under a subscription
they already picked — rather than a metered call the framework made on its own initiative
because a schedule fired. A person doing it by hand still sends nothing at all.

## Verified

- `npm test` passes; `test/seats.test.mjs` gains the `report:` defaults and four refusals,
  including the one that matters — **`report.branch` may not be a seat's workspace**, or the
  round would write a generated page into a branch a seat is told it owns.
- Ran a real round against `constellation.anecdote.channel`: both seats seated, work orders
  written and pushed, digest rendered. See that repo's `council` branch.

## Left undone, deliberately

- **No test for `round.sh` or `publish.sh`.** They are shell over git and an agent; testing
  them honestly means a scratch repo and a fake agent. Worth doing, not done here.
- `order.mjs` derives the method path from `basename(engine-dir)`, so running the engine from
  a checkout that is not named `.advocate-engine` writes a work order pointing at a path the
  next reader does not have. Harmless when mounted normally; noted, not fixed.
- Nothing here schedules anything. The plist is on my machine, not in the repo.

